Let the caller say how much memory a LUKS unlock needs.

The engine gives every LUKS mount 2560 MiB. cryptsetup's key derivation can
want a great deal of memory and there is no way to know how much without asking
the volume, so the engine asks nobody and takes the largest figure it is likely
to need. That is a defensible thing for a program run one drive at a time from
a terminal.

It is not defensible for an application that opens a dozen at once. Each drive
is its own machine, so the figure is not a total but a multiplier, and twelve
of them at 2560 MiB is a number no laptop has. It is also, in the ordinary
case, about eleven times too large: a default `cryptsetup luksFormat` writes a
header asking for 221 MiB, and the volume records what it wants in a place
anybody can read.

Reading it is the caller's job rather than the engine's, because the caller has
already read the first sector to identify the drive and does not want a second
pass over a device it has not been given permission to open. So the floor
becomes something the caller can name, and the constant stays exactly as it was
for anybody who names nothing -- an engine run from a terminal, or a header
Lukotta could not parse, lands where it always did.

The variable is read rather than passed as a flag so that no command line
changes: `--ram-size-mib` already exists and means something else, the maximum
rather than the minimum, and a mount that named both would be asking two
questions with one answer.

--- a/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs
+++ b/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs
@@ -529,8 +529,17 @@
